cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "ECOCIDE: A Satellite-Based Evidentiary Framework for War-Time Environmental Crimes"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Maske"
    given-names: "Sakshi D."
repository-code: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/ECOCIDE"
url: "https://github.com/sakshimaske303-commits/ECOCIDE"
abstract: >-
  A causal-inference framework applying Difference-in-Differences analysis,
  placebo testing, quarterly event-study validation, and exact
  randomization inference to estimate an event-associated vegetation
  decline following the destruction of Ukraine's Kakhovka Dam (6 June
  2023). Comparing monthly NDVI in the conflict-affected Kherson Oblast
  against a matched non-conflict control panel of four Danube/Black Sea
  Romanian counties (Tulcea, Galați, Brăila, Constanța), the primary
  specification (Kherson against Tulcea), using NDVI extracted against each
  zone's true administrative polygon with pixel-level cloud/shadow/snow
  masking, estimates a directionally consistent vegetation decline
  (coefficient -0.0747, HAC-robust p = 0.060) that does not clear the
  conventional 5% significance threshold. A broad-window placebo test
  remains clean. A companion randomization-inference check finds Kherson is
  not the most extreme of the five geographic units available to this
  design, with two of the four Romanian control counties independently
  showing comparable-or-larger shifts of their own; the project reports
  this limitation directly rather than the earlier, more confident-sounding
  version of this result. A verified multi-sensor UNOSAT flood-extent
  timeline is included alongside the vegetation analysis. The project is
  designed to fill a specific, acknowledged gap in existing satellite-based
  assessments of environmental war damage, which rely on qualitative
  interpretation and explicitly decline to establish statistical causality
  — including the gap of disclosing when a corrected analysis weakens an
  earlier, published result.
keywords:
  - ecocide
  - remote sensing
  - causal inference
  - difference-in-differences
  - war crimes
  - satellite evidence
  - Kakhovka Dam
  - NDVI
  - geospatial analysis
